# Knowledge Sandbox

Knowledge Sandbox is a local-first product for inventorying knowledge assets across one or more directories. It produces a portable HTML dashboard and offers an optional read-only REST API for asset discovery, ontology profiles, and SPARQL queries. It is fully standalone: no `onto_field`, database, or cloud service is required.

## Features

- Recursive, read-only scans of configured folders.
- Searchable static dashboard with ontology health and schema details.
- RDF/OWL/Turtle/JSON-LD parsing, including triples, classes, properties, namespaces, and predicates.
- Identification and lightweight metadata for PDF, images, spreadsheets, Word files, presentations, Markdown, CSV/TSV, and archives.
- FastAPI service with Swagger/OpenAPI documentation.
- Fault-tolerant inspection: malformed or unreadable files remain visible with an inspection note.

## Requirements

- Python 3.9+
- Read access to the configured knowledge-base directories

## Quick start

```powershell
cd D:\ss_ai_workspace\openai\projects\onto_field_v6\knowledge_sandbox
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
python -m kmsandbox.cli build sandbox.yaml
```

Open `dist/knowledge_sandbox.html` afterwards.

Use `python -m kmsandbox.cli` as the default command: it works even where Python Scripts is absent from `PATH` or application policy blocks generated console `.exe` shims. When console scripts work, `knowledge-sandbox build sandbox.yaml` is equivalent.

## Configure a knowledge base

```yaml
sandbox:
  title: "Research Knowledge Management Sandbox"
  directories:
    - "D:/knowledge-base"
    - "./examples"
  output: "./dist"
  exclude_patterns: [".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", "dist"]

  template:
    product_name: "Research Knowledge Hub"
    product_tagline: "Research intelligence · local knowledge inventory"
    introduction: "A governed map of research documents and semantic assets."
    primary_color: "#55E5BD"
    accent_color: "#A98BFF"
    footer_html: "<small>Internal use only</small>"
```

Relative paths are resolved from the configuration file. `footer_html` is deliberately rendered as HTML; only use trusted values.

## Dashboard

The HTML dashboard provides summary cards for assets, ontologies, RDF triples, and classes; a full-text filter; an ontology filter; and ontology-level parse health, schema counts, sampled classes, predicates, and namespaces. The static report embeds its inventory metadata, so review it before sharing outside your organization.

## Recognised asset types

| Group | Formats | Metadata collected |
|---|---|---|
| Ontologies | Turtle, RDF/XML, OWL, JSON-LD, N3, N-Triples, TriG | triples, classes, properties, namespaces, predicate samples |
| PDF | `.pdf` | page count |
| Images | PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, BMP, TIFF | dimensions, colour mode, frames |
| Spreadsheets | XLSX, XLS, CSV, TSV | XLSX worksheet names; CSV/TSV headers and column count |
| Documents | DOCX, DOC, RTF, TXT, Markdown | DOCX paragraphs; Markdown headings and lines |
| Presentations | PPTX | slide count |
| Archives | ZIP, 7z | ZIP item count |

Unknown extensions remain in the inventory as `Other file` assets.

## Build the static dashboard

```powershell
python -m kmsandbox.cli build sandbox.yaml
python -m kmsandbox.cli build sandbox.yaml --output .\reports --filename research-hub.html
```

## Run the REST API

```powershell
python -m kmsandbox.cli serve sandbox.yaml --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000
```

Open [http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs](http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs) for interactive API documentation.

| Endpoint | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `GET /api/health` | Status, scan timestamp, and summary |
| `GET /api/assets?kind=Ontology&search=study` | Filtered asset inventory |
| `GET /api/assets/{asset_id}` | One asset and metadata |
| `GET /api/ontologies` | Semantic assets only |
| `GET /api/ontologies/{asset_id}/schema` | Ontology profile |
| `POST /api/query` | Read-only SPARQL `SELECT` / `ASK` |
| `POST /api/rescan` | Refresh configured folders |

### Query example

Request `GET /api/ontologies`, copy an `id`, then run:

```powershell
$body = @{
  asset_id = "YOUR_ASSET_ID"
  scope = "asset"
  query = "SELECT ?subject ?predicate ?object WHERE { ?subject ?predicate ?object }"
} | ConvertTo-Json

Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri "http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/query?limit=50" -ContentType "application/json" -Body $body
```

Use `scope: asset` by default. Use `scope: combined` only for deliberate cross-ontology queries.

## Security boundaries

- The default host is `127.0.0.1`. Add authentication and network controls before exposing the service remotely.
- Only `SELECT` and `ASK` queries are accepted. Updates, remote `SERVICE`, and graph-changing operations are blocked.
- Query responses are capped at 500 rows.
- APIs use scan-generated asset IDs, not caller-provided filesystem paths.
- Scans do not modify source files.

## Development

```powershell
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
python -m pytest tests -q
```

## Project layout

```text
knowledge_sandbox/
├── kmsandbox/      # scanner, dashboard, API, config, and CLI
├── examples/       # sample semantic and document assets
├── tests/
├── sandbox.yaml
├── requirements.txt
└── pyproject.toml
```

## Troubleshooting

| Problem | Resolution |
|---|---|
| `knowledge-sandbox` is not recognized | Use `python -m kmsandbox.cli build sandbox.yaml`. |
| A directory is skipped | Correct the path in `sandbox.yaml`. |
| Ontology status is `attention` | Verify RDF syntax and serialization/extension. |
| A file has an inspection note | Check that it is readable, supported, and not corrupt or password-protected. |

